## Body

Ran into this while trying to turn on `sql_coverage` for a service that uses a query-builder wrapper on top of `Query` (dynamic filters/sort/pagination appended at runtime, so the parameter count isn't known at compile time — hence `ParameterStore` instead of the variadic `Execute(query, args...)`).

`test_sql_coverage` reported literally every single query as uncovered, even ones that run hundreds of times across the test suite. Took a while to track down, but it comes down to `Cluster::Execute(flags, const Query&, const ParameterStore&)` dropping the query's name before it reaches the connection layer:

```cpp
// postgresql/src/storages/postgres/cluster.cpp
ResultSet Cluster::Execute(
    ClusterHostTypeFlags flags, OptionalCommandControl statement_cmd_ctl,
    const Query& query, const ParameterStore& store
) {
    if (!statement_cmd_ctl && query.GetOptionalNameView()) {
        statement_cmd_ctl = GetQueryCmdCtl(*query.GetOptionalNameView());
    }
    statement_cmd_ctl = GetHandlersCmdCtl(statement_cmd_ctl);
    auto ntrx = Start(flags, statement_cmd_ctl);
    return ntrx.Execute(statement_cmd_ctl, query.GetStatementView(), store);
}
```

The `GetQueryCmdCtl(*query.GetOptionalNameView())` call is a bit of a red herring at first glance — it does read the name, but only as a lookup key for per-query timeout overrides, and `CommandControl` is just `{network_timeout_ms, statement_timeout_ms, prepared_statements_enabled}`, nothing that could carry a name onward. The actual loss is on the last line: `query.GetStatementView()` passes along a `zstring_view` — just the raw SQL text — instead of the `Query` object itself. That's the exact point where the name becomes unrecoverable, since nothing downstream ever sees `query` again, only its text.

It gets finalized one level down, in `NonTransaction::Execute`:

```cpp
// postgresql/src/storages/postgres/detail/non_transaction.cpp
ResultSet NonTransaction::Execute(
    OptionalCommandControl statement_cmd_ctl, zstring_view statement, const ParameterStore& store
) {
    return DoExecute(std::string{statement}, ..., statement_cmd_ctl);
}
```

`DoExecute` takes a `const Query&`, so `std::string{statement}` (just a copy of the characters) implicitly converts via `Query(std::string, std::optional<Name> name = std::nullopt, ...)` — and since no name is passed here, it defaults to `std::nullopt`. So this builds a brand new, anonymous `Query`, completely unrelated to the original named one.

By the time it gets to `ConnectionImpl::ExecuteCommand`, `query.GetOptionalNameView()` is empty and the `sql_statement` testpoint (which `sql_coverage` listens for) never fires. Same story for `userver_pg_ntrx`'s fault-injection testpoint (`pg_ntrx_execute::<name>`), since that's gated on the same name check.

The sibling variadic overload — `Cluster::Execute(flags, Query, Args...)` — doesn't have this problem, it forwards the whole `Query` object (`ntrx.Execute(statement_cmd_ctl, query, args...)`), name included. So this really does look specific to the `ParameterStore` path.

I double-checked it's not something on our end — a raw `cluster_->Execute(ClusterHostType::kMaster, kSomeNamedQuery, ParameterStore{}.PushBack(...))` call, with none of our own wrapper code involved, still produces zero `sql_statement` testpoint calls, even though the query has a name and executes fine. Tested against `libuserver-all-dev 3.1`.

Not sure if this was ever exercised through `sql_coverage` before — couldn't find any sample or functional test in the repo that combines `ParameterStore` with `sql_coverage`/testsuite at all (the only `ParameterStore` usages outside the library itself are in the internal gtest-based `*_pgtest.cpp` files, which don't go through testsuite). So it's possible this just fell through the cracks rather than being a deliberate choice.

Seems like a pretty small fix — add an overload to `NonTransaction::Execute` that takes `const Query&` instead of `zstring_view` alongside `ParameterStore`, and have `Cluster::Execute` call that instead of `query.GetStatementView()`:

```cpp
// non_transaction.hpp/.cpp
ResultSet Execute(OptionalCommandControl cmd_ctl, const Query& query, const ParameterStore& store) {
    return DoExecute(query, detail::QueryParameters{store.GetInternalData()}, cmd_ctl);
}
// cluster.cpp
return ntrx.Execute(statement_cmd_ctl, query, store);  // instead of query.GetStatementView()
```

Haven't checked whether `Transaction::Execute(..., ParameterStore)` has the same issue — didn't need to chase that path for our case, but might be worth a look too.

Doesn't affect anything in production (TESTPOINT is a no-op outside testsuite), so low severity — just a bit of a trap, since `sql_coverage` gives no hint about *why* something's uncovered, and a service built around `ParameterStore` (which is exactly what it's for) can never get a clean run.

We've been asked multiple times in the past for NoPorts app mobile support.

@cconstab has an experimental branch: `cconstab/mobile` that he's found to work good on android/ios inherently.

We believe that the work in https://github.com/atsign-foundation/noports/pull/2816 (that migrates the underlying packages to at_client_flutter) will make "remove the cruft" and make things like onboarding more seamless.

We believe that once merged, there won't be much work to get mobile working, so I will mark this ticket as "P4 informational" for now until more work is realized.

